Karere #173 — repost the continuation when CEF's external pump runs out of time slice.

MessagePumpExternal is built with max_time_slice_ = 0.01f. Run() loops over
DirectRunWork() and breaks as soon as 10ms of wall time has passed — but when it
breaks while DoWork() still reported immediate work, it never calls
ScheduleWork(), so the host is never told to pump again.

Chromium cannot recover from that on its own: DoWork() already returned an
immediate NextWorkInfo, so WorkDeduplicator sits in kDoWorkPending and suppresses
every later ScheduleWork() until a DoWork actually runs. Both sides wait for the
other, and the browser only moves again when the embedder happens to pump on a
fallback timer.

Karere hits this on the software off-screen rendering path, where a full-window
blit overruns the slice on nearly every pump: the page animated at ~60 updates/s
while GTK received ~10 draws/s, with a 100ms p95 pump gap matching Karere's
fallback timer exactly. The GPU (DMA-BUF) path keeps pumps near 0.65ms p95, never
overruns the slice, and does not stall.

DirectRunWork() already computes the flag; it just discards it. next_run_time
cannot substitute for it, since it is left null both when immediate work remains
and when there is no work at all. Return it and repost before breaking.

Karere works around this in the embedder: src/cef_pump.rs requests another
main-context turn after each delivered frame, so it does not depend on this
patch. That only covers continuations the app can see coming, though -- a slice
overrun with no frame behind it still strands the pump. Fixing it where the
flag is computed covers every case, and belongs upstream.

Apply from the Chromium src root: patch -p1 < karere-173-pump-continuation.patch

diff --git a/cef/libcef/browser/browser_message_loop.cc b/cef/libcef/browser/browser_message_loop.cc
index 072a176cc..9d1e4a2b7 100644
--- a/cef/libcef/browser/browser_message_loop.cc
+++ b/cef/libcef/browser/browser_message_loop.cc
@@ -34,7 +34,7 @@ class MessagePumpExternal : public base::MessagePumpForUI {
 #endif
 
       base::TimeTicks next_run_time;  // is_null()
-      DirectRunWork(delegate, &next_run_time);
+      const bool more_immediate_work = DirectRunWork(delegate, &next_run_time);
 
       if (next_run_time.is_null()) {
         // We have more work that should run immediately.
@@ -43,6 +43,14 @@ class MessagePumpExternal : public base::MessagePumpForUI {
 
       const base::TimeDelta& delta = next_run_time - start;
       if (delta.InSecondsF() > max_time_slice_) {
+        // KARERE #173: giving up mid-slice with immediate work still queued
+        // strands the embedder. DoWork() already returned an immediate
+        // NextWorkInfo, so WorkDeduplicator is in kDoWorkPending and will
+        // suppress every later ScheduleWork() until a DoWork runs — which only
+        // happens once the host pumps again. Ask for that pump ourselves.
+        if (more_immediate_work) {
+          ScheduleWork();
+        }
         break;
       }
     }
@@ -60,8 +68,9 @@ class MessagePumpExternal : public base::MessagePumpForUI {
   }
 
  private:
-  static void DirectRunWork(Delegate* delegate,
-                            base::TimeTicks* next_run_time) {
+  // Returns true if DoWork() reported work that is ready to run right away.
+  static bool DirectRunWork(Delegate* delegate,
+                            base::TimeTicks* next_run_time) {
     bool more_immediate_work = false;
     bool more_delayed_work = false;
 
@@ -82,6 +91,8 @@ class MessagePumpExternal : public base::MessagePumpForUI {
     if (!more_immediate_work && !more_delayed_work) {
       delegate->DoIdleWork();
     }
+
+    return more_immediate_work;
   }
 
   const float max_time_slice_;
